Well, yes, I did in in 3.1.1.  I guess 3.1.0 wasn't enough.

I'm still very, very frustrated with this game.

This post gives some fairly direct spoilers, so....


This time was a knight.  They don't start off with much strength, so
it was a challenge.

I had a really tight squeeze near the beginning.  I'd just taken off
all my armor to id a bunch of scrolls, when a cockatrice came along.
Well, I started "slowing down," so I knew I was in trouble.
Fortunately, I had met a lizard before that, so I survived.

However, I was still slowed.  Every move took two turns.  Not good.  I
blundered around in the town for a bit when I saw a dwarf zap himself
with a wand of speed monster.  Hey!  I need that!  He was quickly
skewered on my +3 spear.

No wand.

Uh, oh.  It must have been that dwarf KING zapping himself.

The king did not skewer.  In fact, he got rather upset, and at twice
normal speed.  The rothe that came along at the same time didn't help
at all.  I got down to 5 hit points rather quickly.

I had prayed recently to make holy water, so divine intervention was
out.  I looked through my inventory.  I found a wand of digging!!
Kerzap!  Straight down.  Hello next level.  Always have an escape
hatch, even if you have to make it yourself.

I waited awhile until the HP's got back up, and visited the king
again, first dispatching the rothe.  He didn't survive this time, and
the wand quickly restored Alexander to his speedy self.

I really wanted to play with Grayswandir this game, and the plethora
of lawful artifacts makes this difficult.  I resolved to skip the
quest until I had the desired weapon.  The new code in 3.1.1 means
that if you get two artifacts, wishing for another can fail.  However,
the spear eventually wasn't enough, and I had to get Excalibur (which
was).  This was a problem, as no long sword appeared in the game.
Finally, a barrow wight showed up.  Unfortunately, I got Snickersnee
right after that from a bones level.

I blundered onto a magic lamp right before the castle.  I really
wanted Grayswandir, but I went for the speed boots instead.  Good
choice.

I managed to take out Fort Ludios early on.  The bones pile did give
me one good thing:  a wand of polymorph with two charges left on it.
I used the wand to get a cloak of displacement and helms of telepathy
and opposite aligment.  I also got a cloak of magic resistance which I
put on immediately.  The gauntlets of power I just found (no
polypiling needed).

Strategy for fort:  Lure monsters into the room with the gold and
let _them_ get blown to bits.

One of the white dragons in the fort left scales, so I made dragon
scale mail from that.  It is so much lighter than metal armor, it is
worth doing from the first set of scales you find.

I never did find a scroll of genocide in the whole game, so the castle
was rough.  I managed to turn two liches and a demilich, but I had to
dice the master lich by hand.  I didn't bother playing music as the
drawbridge is very dangerous.  Instead, I blasted the doorway with a
force bolt spell and used a ring of levitation I found to get over.

I found out that rust monsters can swim.  The hard way.

Fortunately, I had lots of identifies, so the scroll of charging was
still in hand when I got to the wand.  That took quite awhile with no
stealth and no conflict.  I forgot how tough the castle can be!

The first attempt at wishing for Grayswandir failed.  I had two
artifacts at this point (Excalibur and Snickersnee), so the chances
were still pretty good.  I tried again and got it.

The third wish went to a blessed scroll of genocide.  Goodbye liches,
forever, and good riddance.  I'd already slain quite a few, and they
were getting to be a pain.

Someone once posted that you can wish for a magic lamp, get an
infinite light source, and still have the wish for when you really
need it.

No you can't.

Fortunately, I _found_ a magic lamp a little way below the castle.  I
never used the wish in it, and kept the light source for the whole
game.

The quest was uneventful.  The Magic Mirror isn't very useful compared
to the Valkyrie, Tourist, or Samurai artifact, but it came in handy.
I got rid of the helm of telepathy and used a helm of brilliance
instead.  I got rid of the cloak of magic resistance and used one of
protection instead.

Grayswandir enchanted to +6 (I was hoping for +7, or maybe even +8).
It was _nice_.  Ice devils, shades, and vampires all died in one or
two blows.  Pit fiends always died in two blows.  For whatever reason,
dragons _always_ took seven hits.  Every time.  I counted.  I hung
onto Excalibur for exploring, as it helped avoid traps.  I usually
switched to Grayswandir for combat.  Potent combination, that.

I had the hardest time getting shock resistance.  The blue dragon I
kilt in Ludios didn't leave a corpse.  This is the first time this
happened to me and I was quite surprised.

For a really good time, I went to the Valley of the Dead and tried
turning the nearest zombies.  _That_ was much more exciting than I
intended, but I survived, thanks to the speed boots (run away!  run
away!).

I was curious to see if the Bell abuse really works.  It does.  That
bit really should be removed from the game.  For a priest or knight,
it is ridiculous.

The wiz was easy.  I used a wand to teleport away his eels.  They
don't die of this, but they do go down to 1 HP, and they can't pull
you into water.

The inner sanctum was also much more exciting than I intended.  The
Wizard chose this moment to show up.  He stole my Mirror.  Excuse me?
You aren't allowed to do that!  That was my magic protection!  Now I'm
a sitting duck!  Some strange foresight led me to keep that cloak of
magic resistance in the bag of holding.  I quickly slipped it on.
Whew!

Then, I just _couldn't_ find the last secret door, even with Excalibur
helping me.  I tried the stethoscope.  I tried banging away with the
pickaxe.  Nothing!  I'd earlier ditched my wand of secret door
detection, thinking it useless.  I'd also never found the right
spellbook.  Oh dear.  Well, I _had_ found another magic lamp, so I
used it to conjure up a wand of secret door detection.  That did it.
I think that particular secret door is unfindable without magic.

I'd left a bag of holding with about 100 jewels and 95K gold right by
the out staircase.  A lot better than dragging it around the dungeon.
the rest of the game was slower, though.

Earth was easy.  I'd found two crystal balls.  It turned out that both
were cursed, and one was out of charges.  I dropped the empty one and
uncursed the other.  That found me the portal.  A wand of digging took
me right there.

As soon as I got to Air, I genocided dragons.  I should have done that
long before.  Another peek in the ball, and out of there!

Fire was tough!  The crystal ball was out of charges.  I didn't have
any charging scrolls, and no blank paper, either.  I used the Amulet
and wandered around a long time before finding the way out.  There
didn't seem to be as many archons as in 3.1.0.

Water was _really_ tough.  I used a wish to get a cursed scroll of
gold detection.  That didn't help, as the $ symbol disappeared.
I wandered around there with the help of an amulet of magical
breathing and a ring of levitation.  I don't know which one kept me
from drowning, and I don't really care...  I still couldn't find the
portal.  Finally, I used the 'worn out wand' wish to get another crystal
ball.  The ^ symbol disappeared, too!  I fooled around in the bubble
until I saw it again.  It was moving!  I finally managed to chase it
down.  With how long it took, good think I genocided eels as soon as I
got there.

Astral wasn't much of a problem in regards to the monsters.  I quickly
ditched extra items to lose weight and gain inventory slots.  I
discovered that teleport doesn't work there any more.  To my surprise,
magic mapping doesn't work either!

I wanted to ascend with a pet, so I let my vampire lord out of his
figurine.  He nailed an angel for me, then died.  Oops.  In the first
entryway, I got quite hemmed in by bugs that came from somewhere plus
two angels, so I read a blessed scroll of taming.  The bugs didn't do
much besided eat corpses, but the angels became peaceful (not tame).

I found I had one very big problem.  Food.  The monsters couldn't kill
me directly, but they could keep me from eating.  I had to eat fast
because of the Amulet.  I made it through the first room and found
myself weak.  Well, I'll lock the door behind myself to keep out those
angels, then eat.

Oops, dropped my key back there.  No time to go back for it.

There was no help for it.  I cast Wizard Lock.  And fainted from
hunger as a result.  This isolated me long enough....

Now I was in really big trouble, with probably not enough time to eat.
The food was in a bag, which was also in a bag.  The quickie lizard
corpses didn't help much.  I prayed.  At this point, Lugh took mercy
and fed me.  Whew!

The middle turned out to be Lugh's altar.  I stood on it, blew my
whistle to get all my (remaining) pets, and gave the Amulet to Lugh.

Immortality, yes, yes.

Identification:  yes, yes.

Intrinsics:  whew!  a lot!

Ok, here comes the score!!!

Suddenly, the dungeon collapses.

What!?!?!?!!?

There is now a new entry in the worst death of all time.

